NNAAMMEE
     aappmmdd — Advanced Power Management monitor daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aappmmdd [--dd] [--ff --ffiillee] [--ss] [--vv]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aappmmdd utility monitors the occurrence of the specified Advanced Power
     Management (APM) events and, if one of the events occurs, it executes the
     sequence of commands corresponding to the event.  Only the events speci‐
     fied in the configuration file are notified to aappmmdd; all other events are
     ignored.  For each event posted by the APM BIOS, aappmmdd invokes the se‐
     quence of commands specified in the configuration file.  When aappmmdd is
     running with monitoring suspend/standby requests, the kernel will not
     process those requests.  Therefore, if you wish action to be taken when
     these events occur, you need to explicitly configure the appropriate com‐
     mands or built-in functions in the configuration file.

     The aappmmdd utility recognizes the following runtime options:

     --dd      Starts in debug mode.  This causes aappmmdd to execute in the fore‐
             ground instead of in daemon mode.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Specifies a different configuration file _f_i_l_e to be used in place
             of the default _/_e_t_c_/_a_p_m_d_._c_o_n_f.

     --ss      Causes aappmmdd to simulate a POWERSTATECHANGE event when a power
             state change is detected (AC_POWER_STATE) but the bios of the
             laptop does not report it.  This enables you to do things like
             dimming the LCD backlight when you unplug the power cord.

     --vv      Verbose mode.

     When aappmmdd starts, it reads the configuration file (_/_e_t_c_/_a_p_m_d_._c_o_n_f as de‐
     fault) and notifies the set of events to be monitored to the APM device
     driver.  When it terminates, the APM device driver automatically cancels
     monitored events.

     If the aappmmdd process receives a SIGHUP, it will reread its configuration
     file and notify the APM device driver of any changes to its configura‐
     tion.

     The aappmmdd utility uses the device _/_d_e_v_/_a_p_m_c_t_l to issue ioctl(2) requests
     for monitoring events and for controlling the APM system.  This device
     file is opened exclusively, so only a single aappmmdd process can be running
     at any time.

     When aappmmdd receives an APM event, it forks a child process to execute the
     commands specified in the configuration file and then continues listening
     for more events.  The child process executes the commands specified, one
     at a time and in the order that they are listed.

     While aappmmdd is processing the command list for SUSPEND/STANDBY requests,
     the APM kernel device driver issues notifications to APM BIOS once per
     second so that the BIOS knows that there are still some commands pending,
     and that it should not complete the request just yet.

     The aappmmdd utility creates the file _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_a_p_m_d_._p_i_d, and stores its
     process id there.  This can be used to kill or reconfigure aappmmdd.

CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN FFIILLEE
     The structure of the aappmmdd configuration file is quite simple.  For exam‐
     ple:

     apm_event SUSPENDREQ {
            exec "sync && sync && sync";
            exec "sleep 1";
            exec "zzz";
     }

     will cause aappmmdd to receive the APM event ‘SUSPENDREQ’ (which may be
     posted by an LCD close), run the ‘sync’ command 3 times and wait for a
     while, then execute zzzzzz (aappmm --zz) to put the system in the suspend state.

     ••   The apm_event keyword

               ‘apm_event’ is the keyword which indicates the start of config‐
               uration for each event.

     ••   APM events

               If you wish to execute the same commands for different events,
               the event names should be delimited by a comma.  The following
               are valid event names:

               - Events ignored by the kernel if aappmmdd is running:

                     STANDBYREQ
                     USERSTANDBYREQ
                     SUSPENDREQ      should include sync in the command list,
                     USERSUSPENDREQ  should include sync in the command list,
                     BATTERYLOW      only zzz should be specified in the com‐
                                     mand list.

               - Events passed to aappmmdd after kernel handling:

                     NORMRESUME
                     CRITRESUME
                     STANDBYRESUME
                     POWERSTATECHANGE
                     UPDATETIME
                     CAPABILITIESCHANGE

               Other events will not be sent to aappmmdd.

     ••   command line syntax

               In the example above, the three lines beginning with ‘exec’ are
               commands for the event.  Each line should be terminated with a
               semicolon.  The command list for the event should be enclosed
               by ‘{’ and ‘}’.  The aappmmdd utility uses _/_b_i_n_/_s_h for double-quo‐
               tation enclosed command execution, just as with system(3).
               Each command is executed in order until the end of the list is
               reached or a command finishes with a non-zero status code.  The
               aappmmdd utility will report any failed command's status code via
               syslog(3) and will then reject the request event posted by the
               APM BIOS.

     ••   Built-in functions

               You can also specify aappmmdd built-in functions instead of command
               lines.  A built-in function name should be terminated with a
               semicolon, just as with a command line.  The following built-in
               functions are currently supported:


               - reject  Reject last request posted by APM BIOS.  This can be
                         used to reject a SUSPEND request when the LCD is
                         closed and put the system in a STANDBY state instead.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/apmd.conf
     /dev/apmctl
     /var/run/apmd.pid

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Sample configuration commands include:

     apm_event SUSPENDREQ {
             exec "/etc/rc.suspend apm suspend";
     }

     apm_event USERSUSPENDREQ {
             exec "sync && sync && sync";
             exec "sleep 1";
             exec "apm -z";
     }

     apm_event NORMRESUME {
             exec "/etc/rc.resume apm suspend";
     }

     apm_event STANDBYRESUME {
             exec "/etc/rc.resume apm standby";
     }

     # resume event configuration for serial mouse users by
     # reinitializing a moused(8) connected to a serial port.
     #
     #apm_event NORMRESUME {
     #       exec "kill -HUP `cat /var/run/moused.pid`";
     #}
     #
     # suspend request event configuration for ATA HDD users:
     # execute standby instead of suspend.
     #
     #apm_event SUSPENDREQ {
     #       reject;
     #       exec "sync && sync && sync";
     #       exec "sleep 1";
     #       exec "apm -Z";
     #}

